\paragraph{Prerequisites.}
Let $P_2(\mathbb R)$ be the three-dimensional real vector space of polynomials of degree at most two. The ordered basis is $\mathcal B=(1,x,x^2)$. A matrix column records the coordinates of the image of the corresponding basis vector. Assume the definitions of eigenvalue, eigenspace, diagonalizability, and inverse linear map. The previously available triangular-matrix theorem states that the eigenvalues of a triangular matrix are precisely its diagonal entries, counted with algebraic multiplicity. The identity operator is denoted by $\id$; differentiation is a linear map on $P_2(\mathbb R)$.
 The reader also knows that a polynomial over $\mathbb R$ with zero derivative is constant.

\paragraph{Prerequisites.}
All functions below map $[0,1]$ into $\mathbb R$. Continuity at an endpoint means continuity relative to $[0,1]$. Uniform convergence means that for every $\eta>0$ there is $N_0$ such that $|f_n(x)-f(x)|<\eta$ for every $n\ge N_0$ and every $x\in[0,1]$. Assume the triangle inequality, continuity of polynomials, and $x^n\to0$ when $0\le x<1$.
 The quantifier $\forall$ is familiar.
